+## Zarr storage
+
+When an `AnnData` object is written to Zarr (e.g. via `write_zarr` or `AnnData.write_zarr`),
+the same element encodings described below are used, but the container is a Zarr store
+instead of an HDF5 file. A Zarr store is a directory (or a store backed by another
+hierarchical key-value system) whose keys form a tree, mirroring the groups and arrays
+of the HDF5 layout.
+
+Each group in the tree is represented by a directory containing a `.zgroup` file,
+and each array is a directory containing a `.zarray` file (describing shape, dtype and
+chunking) plus one or more chunk files. The `encoding-type` and `encoding-version`
+metadata described below are stored in the group's or array's `.zattrs` file.
+
+For example, an `AnnData` object written to Zarr has a tree structure like:
+
+```
+adata.zarr
+├── .zgroup
+├── .zattrs                 # {'encoding-type': 'anndata', 'encoding-version': '0.1.0'}
+├── X
+│   ├── .zgroup
+│   ├── .zattrs             # {'encoding-type': 'csr_matrix', 'encoding-version': '0.1.0', 'shape': [...]}
+│   ├── data
+│   ├── indices
+│   └── indptr
+├── obs
+│   ├── .zgroup
+│   ├── .zattrs             # {'encoding-type': 'dataframe', 'encoding-version': '0.2.0', ...}
+│   └── ...
+├── var
+│   └── ...
+├── layers
+│   └── ...
+├── obsm
+│   └── ...
+├── varm
+│   └── ...
+├── obsp
+│   └── ...
+├── varp
+│   └── ...
+└── uns
+    └── ...
+```
+
+You can inspect this tree with Zarr's `tree()` method:
+
+```python
+>>> import zarr
+>>> z = zarr.open("adata.zarr", "r")
+>>> z.tree()
+```
+
+The element specifications in the following sections apply equally to Zarr and HDF5,
+with the Zarr-specific notes called out where the two formats differ
+(e.g. for string scalars and string arrays).
